id: rust.connection-pool
kind: rust
title: Database connection pool
aliases: pool, sqlx pool, connection management
tags: database, performance, resilience
questions:
  - how do you size a connection pool?
invariant: A pool bounds concurrent database work, so pool size is a backpressure decision rather than a performance dial.
answer_30s: Build one pool at startup, size it from the database's capacity (not from request count), set an acquire timeout, and treat pool wait time as a metric.
answer_2m: |
  Reasoning: each connection consumes database memory and a scheduler slot, so
  more connections can make the database slower. Postgres practice suggests a
  small number of connections relative to database cores, and queueing in the
  application (where you can shed load) rather than inside the database (where
  you cannot). max_connections must be below the database limit divided by the
  number of replicas. The acquire timeout matters more than it looks: without
  it, a saturated pool turns into unbounded request latency; with it, saturation
  becomes a fast, visible, shedable error.
when_to_use:
  - every service talking to a database or broker with expensive connections
failure_modes:
  - a pool per request or per handler, defeating pooling
  - max_connections multiplied across replicas exceeding the database limit
  - no acquire timeout, so database slowness becomes an unbounded request queue
related:
  - rust.app-state
  - design.connection-pool
  - design.backpressure
  - recipe.database-backed-api
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/sqlx/latest/sqlx/struct.Pool.html
